00:00:02.639 --> 00:00:03.350 align:start position:0%
 
hi there  steve kaufman uh i'm gonna continue here  i got a bit of a bee in my bonnet this  morning i  sat in on a on an e teachers  conference put put on by kirsten winkler  who describes herself as a professional  education 2.0 blogger interviewer and  edupreneur she is the founder of the  e-teachers academy and she hosts the  e-teachers conference  and so today i sat in on a conference  that had dwayne cider director of  communications from rosetta stone  shiv rad gendron operations director for  languagelab.com christopher grant  senior vice president of sklipo.com  sclipo.com  and stefan stephenson founder and ceo of  mingleville  so four people who are significant  in so far as language learning on the  web is concerned  and they used a technology that was  known  as is called whiz ich wiziq.com  and so we were able to sit there and  watch a presentation  using powerpoint slides whatever and on  the side we could scribble our little  comments as the presentation went on  and of course the subject was something  like the future of the teacher  in this world of e-learning  um you know i sometimes wonder about who  has the patience to sit through one of  my  videos for up to 10 minutes  but this e-teachers conference lasted  two hours that's an awfully long time to  sit in front of your screen  i had decided i was going to do it so i  stayed there  i don't think i would go again so  the technology the format the whizzick  wi  ziq i thought it was kind of good i'm  going to look into using it myself  for perhaps seminars on how to use link  or something  but the amount of time is just simply  too long now you could argue that  i went all the way down to san diego to  sit in on conferences  some of which were much worse than the  presentations  that we had here on this screen so in  terms of use of time  this was still relatively more efficient  than going to san diego and walking  around trying to find you know  lecture halls which is probably true  which is probably true  uh insofar as the content oh and the  other thing was it was sort of  couched and presented by by kirsten and  couched in terms of  you know everything is changing which is  true and and the first presenter  who was uh no  he's not christopher grant i guess of of  sklipo  talked about all this sort of new  technology that is changing our  behaviors and all of that is true  and the whole suggestion was that uh  you know the barriers to entry in terms  of creating websites is so  small it's going to be more and more  websites  i would caution you that the cost of  putting together a website  of any substance such as link in fact is  very very high it costs a lot of money  point number one then there was a lot of  talk about  education is a right and education  should be free  and i think the term there was digital  altruism  uh you know i hear this free thing all  the time  uh i think it's really worthwhile trying  to understand what we mean here  most people don't want to work for  nothing  so if free means that the user isn't  paying  all it means is that someone else is  paying  or that the person providing the service  is doing it for free  most programmers don't work for free  most teachers like to get paid  publishers writers managers they all  like to get paid  so either you find government funding  which means  tax money so in other words that the  taxpayers money is going into this thing  whatever you're promoting  rather than going into hospital beds  roads  uh you know or or schools or whatever  so it's not free unless you're  volunteering  now many people volunteer their services  because they have another job but  anybody that's doing something as a  major activity as their  primary activity likes to be paid  because that way  they can buy food and feed their  families so  free we have a right to free someone  should give us something  i don't think so i don't think that we  have any more a right  to free education than we have a right  to free  books shoes clothes  or a trip to hawaii uh these are all  services goods or services that someone  has to produce and the people who  produce them  like to be paid also the idea that  somehow  people on the internet are altruistic i  don't buy for a minute the only  reason that so many things are are free  on the internet  is because that's what the market  dictates because people are struggling  to find ways  to make money on the internet and they  can't afford to spend  hundreds of millions of dollars  promoting their brand so they make it  available free  in the hope that people will like it and  then at some point they can figure out  some way to make money from it  and link is very much in that same  category so i don't believe in digital  altruism  what is going to happen is that the  internet is so efficient  as a means of distributing information  that the additional cost  once something has been created be it a  book or a lesson plan or learning  content or a learning system  the cost of delivering it to two people  or two million people is about the same  so what's going to happen is that the  cost of distributing  services educational services is going  to come way down  and we're going to see an explosion of  people producing educational services  web apps ipod apps and so forth  and so that the idea that the the sort  of prevailing monopoly that your local  university or school has  you have to go there and if you want to  study um you know economics you have to  study with this professor  and what's more his courses full so you  have to take this other course that  you're not even interested in  all of that's going to change you'll be  able to get whatever you want  on the internet and a lot of schools  realize this  and i again i mentioned the a c  t f l american teachers of four  languages  the university of texas at austin has  wonderful resources  that they have put up and have made  available to other people and the idea  is that  those people are in the business of  education they are paid to be in  education  by either the state of texas or private  donors or whatever it might be people  who go to the university of texas they  pay money but they are looking for ways  and they realize that if they put up  content they share with others they can  also share  back from some of the content that  others have put up  so there is going to be a lot more  sharing  and the inner university of texas even  had a program whereby they  prepare courses but they don't print the  textbook and if you want it you print it  on demand  and it's significantly cheaper so  there's all kinds of ways practical ways  that the internet is going to make  education cheaper but there's no  right to education and there is no right  to free education  and there is no digital altruism in my  opinion  that's not to say that we don't have a  you know an altruistic impulse here and  there  which i think comes from the desire that  if we help someone else out they'll help  also so it's still a mutual altruism  uh type of thing uh i am going to do a  separate video where i'm going to talk  about the substance of these four  presentations  in particular the presentation from  dwayne  cider of rosetta stone and i want to  talk about the sort of what i see as the  two  sort of positions on language learning  there's two main positions  which is one our position at link which  is  content words get the words get the  language  in you before you can start to  understand the structure  and the other perspective which is  that of rosetta stone which is that of  grammar teachers and that is that first  we give you the structure  then you can go out and accumulate words  so that will be the subject  of my next video thank you very much for  listening

